steps.body:
A list endpoint that returns everything works with three rooms and breaks with three thousand: the answer gets slow, big, and useless on a phone. Query parameters let a client ask for just what it needs, a filtered, sorted page of the list, and they're where most of the reading-text-into-values work happens.

## Roombook's room filters

`GET /v1/rooms` takes these parameters:

- `building=main-library`: only rooms in that building, named by its **slug**, a short name made for URLs.
- `minCapacity=4`: only rooms that fit at least 4 people.
- `amenity=whiteboard`: only rooms that have it. Repeat it to ask for several, `amenity=whiteboard&amenity=screen`, and a room must have all of them.
- `sort=capacity`: the order, `name` or `capacity`, with `name` as the default.
- `limit=20` and `cursor=...`: the page size and where to start, explained below.

## @RequestParam

The controller declares each one:

```java
@GetMapping
public ResponseEntity<Object> list(
        @RequestParam MultiValueMap<String, String> params,
        @RequestParam(required = false) String building,
        @RequestParam(required = false) Integer minCapacity,
        @RequestParam(name = "amenity", required = false) List<String> amenities,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "name") String sort,
        @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int limit,
        @RequestParam(required = false) String cursor) {
    RoomQuery query;
    try {
        query = RoomQuery.of(params.keySet(), building, minCapacity, amenities, sort, limit, cursor);
    } catch (RoomQuery.InvalidQueryException e) {
        return ApiError.response(HttpStatus.UNPROCESSABLE_CONTENT, e.getMessage());
    }
    return ResponseEntity.ok(rooms.list(query));
}
```

Every parameter is required unless it says `required = false` or has a `defaultValue`, and a missing optional one is `null`, which is why `minCapacity` is an `Integer` and not an `int`. A `List<String>` collects every value of a repeated name, and `name = "amenity"` maps the singular name in the URL to the plural one in Java. The first parameter, a `MultiValueMap`, gets the whole query string too, so the names can be checked. Spring converts `limit` to an `int` before the handler runs, and text that isn't a number gets a 400, as the third example shows.

`RoomQuery.of` checks the rest and builds a typed `RoomQuery` record, or throws `InvalidQueryException`, a small exception of its own:

```java
private static final Set<String> NAMES = Set.of("building", "minCapacity", "amenity", "sort", "limit", "cursor");
private static final Set<String> SORTS = Set.of("name", "capacity");

public static RoomQuery of(Set<String> names, String building, Integer minCapacity, List<String> amenities,
        String sort, int limit, String cursor) {
    for (String name : names) {
        if (!NAMES.contains(name)) {
            throw new InvalidQueryException("Unknown query parameter: " + name);
        }
    }
    if (!SORTS.contains(sort)) {
        throw new InvalidQueryException("sort must be one of: name, capacity");
    }
    if (limit < 1 || limit > 100) {
        throw new InvalidQueryException("limit must be a whole number from 1 to 100");
    }
    Cursor after = null;
    if (cursor != null) {
        after = Cursors.decode(cursor)
                .orElseThrow(() -> new InvalidQueryException("cursor is not one this API gave out"));
    }
    return new RoomQuery(building, minCapacity, amenities == null ? List.of() : amenities, sort, limit, after);
}
```

The controller catches it and answers **422**, since the request was readable but a value broke a rule, with the message as the title, in the same first-draft shape as the 404s from the routes lesson. The lesson on validation and errors replaces this hand-written checking with annotations on the record, and lists every field that's wrong at once.

Four habits are in that code:

- **Defaults.** A missing `sort` means `name`, and a missing `limit` means 20. A client should get a sensible answer from the bare URL.
- **Bounds.** `limit` can't go over 100, or one request could ask for a million rooms and tie up the server building the answer.
- **An allowlist for sorting.** Only names on the list are accepted. In the lesson on raw SQL, the sort becomes part of a query in SQL, the language databases speak, and taking a column name from a client as it is would let them sort by anything, like a password column, or slip in commands of their own.
- **Unknown names are refused.** A typo like `minCapcity` would otherwise be quietly ignored, and the client would get every room and never know why. Roombook answers 422 instead, naming the parameter:

```bash
curl -i 'http://localhost:8080/v1/rooms?colour=blue'
```

```text
HTTP/1.1 422
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: 56
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:00:32 GMT

{"status":422,"title":"Unknown query parameter: colour"}
```

## Pages: offset or cursor

The obvious way to page through a list is an **offset**: `?offset=20&limit=10` means skip 20, then take 10. It's simple, and it breaks when the list changes between two requests, as the top half of the diagram shows:

```diagram request-data-pagination
With an offset, a new item at the front pushes C onto the next page too. A cursor remembers C itself.
```

A **cursor** says where the last page ended instead: start after C. New items arriving earlier in the list can't shift it, and the database can jump straight to the spot, while an offset of 100,000 makes it count through 100,000 rows first.

Roombook's list answers are a `Page` record, with a `nextCursor` next to `items`. The client sends it back as `cursor` to get the next page, and a `null` means there are no more:

```java
public static String encode(Object after, long id) {
    String text = after + "," + id;
    return Base64.getUrlEncoder().withoutPadding().encodeToString(text.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
}
```

The cursor holds the last item's sort value and its id, since two rooms can share a capacity, written in **base64url**, a way to write any bytes with only letters, digits, `-`, and `_`, which are safe in a URL. Clients treat it as an opaque token: they pass it back unchanged and never build one themselves, which leaves Roombook free to change what's inside.

The body is the only place a client can send real content, and the only place where the client controls how much arrives. A careless API reads whatever comes, however big, in whatever format, and that's how one request runs a server out of memory. Roombook's rules for bodies are short and strict, and Spring enforces most of them already.

## JSON only, or 415

`@RequestBody` hands the body to a **message converter**, Spring's code that turns a body into a Java object, picked by the request's `Content-Type`. Roombook has Jackson's converter, which reads `application/json` and every type ending in `+json`, like the merge patch. A body in any other format has no converter, so Spring answers **415 Unsupported Media Type**, I can't read that format, before your handler runs, as the first example shows. The answer even carries an `Accept` header listing the types that would have worked.

## Broken JSON, or 400

If the body says it's JSON but isn't, like one with a missing bracket, Jackson fails to read it and Spring answers **400 Bad Request**, with a `message` starting `JSON parse error`. Both answers come in Spring Boot's own error format for now, and the lesson on validation and errors puts them in Roombook's.

## A size limit, or 413

Here Spring and Tomcat do less than you might expect. Tomcat's `max-http-form-post-size` setting only covers HTML form posts, so a JSON body has no size limit at all by default. A client could send a gigabyte, and Jackson would try to read it all into memory.

Roombook sets its own limit of 100 kilobytes, far more than a booking's 200 bytes, in `common/BodyLimitInterceptor.java`. An **interceptor** is code that Spring MVC runs before each handler, which the middleware lesson covers:

```java
@Override
public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, Object handler)
        throws IOException {
    if (request.getContentLengthLong() > maxBytes) {
        response.setStatus(413);
        response.setContentType("application/json");
        response.getWriter().write("{\"status\":413,\"title\":\"Request body too large\"}");
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```

`WebConfig` registers it with `registry.addInterceptor(new BodyLimitInterceptor(100 * 1024))`. It reads the request's `Content-Length` header and, when it's over the limit, writes **413 Content Too Large** itself and returns `false`, which stops the request before anything reads the body. Returning `true` lets the request carry on. It has one gap: a client can leave `Content-Length` out and send the body in chunks, and the security lesson closes it with a limit at the reverse proxy in front of the API.

## Forms and file uploads

APIs mostly take JSON, but you'll meet two other formats:

- `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` is what an HTML form sends by default, `name=Hamilton&capacity=10`. `@RequestParam` reads its fields.
- `multipart/form-data` carries files, like a photo of a room, in parts. A `MultipartFile` parameter receives one, and `spring.servlet.multipart.max-file-size` limits its size.

A big upload shouldn't sit in memory. `request.getInputStream()` reads it as a **stream**, data that arrives in chunks, so the server can pass each chunk on to storage as it comes, and memory stays small whatever the file's size.

## No body on GET or DELETE

Recall from the method table in the routes lesson: a body on GET or DELETE has no agreed meaning, and many proxies drop it. So Roombook never reads one, and a client that needs to send data to a read uses the query string.

## Missing or null, in a PATCH

The routes lesson left one question open: how does a handler tell a field that's missing from one that's `null`? A booking's note shows why it matters. `{"attendees": 4}` should keep the note, and `{"note": null}` should clear it. A record can't tell them apart, so `PATCH /v1/bookings/{bookingId}` takes the body as a `JsonNode`, the JSON itself, and asks whether each field is there:

```java
@PatchMapping("/{bookingId}")
public BookingView update(@PathVariable String bookingId, @RequestBody JsonNode patch) {
    Booking booking = find(bookingId);
    int attendees = patch.has("attendees") ? patch.get("attendees").asInt() : booking.attendees();
    String note = patch.has("note") ? patch.get("note").stringValue(null) : booking.note();
    return BookingView.from(bookings.update(booking.withChanges(attendees, note)));
}
```

`patch.has("note")` is true when the client sent a `note` field, even one set to `null`, and false when they left it out. `stringValue(null)` gives the text, or `null` when the JSON value isn't text, which is what a JSON `null` becomes. Use this for every field that can be cleared.
